General Rules
All participants must comply with the competition rules and instructions provided by the organisers.
Participants must act fairly and professionally throughout the competition.
Any attempt to disrupt the competition infrastructure, scoring system, or other teams outside the intended game mechanics is strictly prohibited.
The organisers reserve the right to investigate suspicious activities and penalise teams that violate the rules.
 
RE:HACK Infra [RN10],  — 2026/11/13 12:17
Mandatory Pwnbox Usage Strictly enforced
All participants MUST use the Pwnbox environment provided by the organisers to participate in the competition.
Participants are strictly prohibited from using their own:
Personal laptops or computers to perform competition activities.
Virtual machines (VMs).
Cloud instances, VPS, or external servers.
External hacking environments or attack platforms.
Any 3rd party device (flipper-zero, Raspberry Pi, etc)

All competition related activities, including attacking, defending, analysing, and interacting with challenge infrastructure, must be conducted through the provided Pwnbox ONLY!

Participants must not bypass Pwnbox restrictions or establish unauthorised connections through external environments.

The organisers reserve the right to monitor Pwnbox activities to ensure compliance.

Failure to comply may result in disqualification.
RE:HACK Infra [RN10],  — 2026/11/13 12:44
AI Usage Policy
The use of Artificial Intelligence (AI) tools is strictly prohibited throughout the competition.
Participants must not use AI-powered tools or services to assist with any competition-related activities.
Prohibited AI tools include, but are not limited to:
ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini, Microsoft Copilot and DeepSeek.
AI-powered coding assistants such as GitHub Copilot, Cursor and Windsurf.
AI-powered vulnerability analysis and exploitation tools.
AI-powered automated agents or autonomous hacking tools.
Participants must not use AI to generate, modify, debug, or optimise exploit code, scripts, payloads, or defensive solutions.
Participants must not submit competition challenges, source code, binaries, network traffic, or flags to AI services for analysis.
The following activities are strictly prohibited:
Using AI to identify or analyse vulnerabilities.
Generating exploits, payloads, or attack scripts using AI.
Using AI to troubleshoot or debug competition-related code.
Using AI to analyse captured network traffic or challenge binaries.
Using AI to automate attacks or defensive operations.
Using AI to search for solutions or hints to competition challenges.
Using AI through APIs, browser extensions, plugins, or third-party integrations.

Additional rules, clarifications, restrictions, and competition-related updates may be announced by the organisers on the event day. 
RE:HACK Infra [RN10],  — 2026/11/13 13:46
Please take note of the following rules for the final round:

Each team is allowed to have only up to 3 active players during the competition.
The 4th player will serve as a substitute.
Substitutions are allowed only between Phase 1 and Phase 2.
No player changes or substitutions are allowed while a phase/game is in progress.
Player 4 (substitute) is not allowed to communicate with active players or share any challenge-related information, hints, solutions, flags, or exploits during an ongoing phase.
Please ensure your team finalises its lineup before the competition begins.
RE:HACK Infra [RN10],  — 17:05
DONT DO THIS!!
DDoS (Distributed Denial of Service) by flooding a service or network with excessive traffic to disrupt availability. For automation script, put delay 2sec atleast.
Unauthorized Network Sniffing. Your team only can capture or monitoring network traffic inside your assigned competition environment. (you have control only).
Executing processes designed to exhaust system resources, including CPU, memory, or process limits. (Fork Bomb)
Disrupting Other Players' Services: Intentionally crashing, disabling, exploiting infrastructure, or otherwise interfering with services belonging to other teams.
Scanning, enumeration, or probing of other teams' services.. (everything already provided. Not allowed to run any port scanning, directory scanning etc)
Change configuration in Firewall, not limited to:
-- modifying firewall rules.
-- adding IP-based restrictions to block other teams.
-- dropping or rejecting connections from competing teams
-- manipulating network filtering to prevent legitimate competition traffic.
-- changing firewall policies to gain an unfair competitive advantage. 

RE:HACK Clarifications (UiTM Venue & Tooling):
1. UiTM only provides each team with 1 LAN cable and 1 plug point. Bring your own router/switch and power extensions.
2. External monitors are allowed.
3. Phase 1 and Phase 2 are different, but both must use the provided Pwnbox.
4. Pre-existing automation tools/scripts (e.g., RsaCtfTool, linpeas, Wireshark, and AI-assisted scripts written BEFORE the competition) CAN be brought into Pwnbox, as long as they do not connect to any AI/MCP and human interaction ("human touch") is still used. During the competition, using AI is strictly prohibited.